## Changes

- 2026-10-19 MCP connect control: per-server switch in McpPanel -> `mcp.toggle` -> `McpManager.set_server_enabled_state` (idempotent, non-blocking submit onto the WS loop). Disabled list moved to the global user scope (`~/.pebble/disabled_mcp_servers.json`), legacy per-project file read as fallback. `mcp.list` now reports `enabled` / `state` from the merged config and hides unusable placeholder entries unless `mcp_install.json` declares them installable. `mcp.reload` re-applies config changes at runtime (added / removed / changed). State changes are pushed over WS as `mcp.state_changed`
- 2026-10-19 code_agent prompt: removed the duplicated `## Available tools` list (the API `tools` schema is authoritative); section renamed to `## Tool notes`, keeping the Windows execution notes, plan-mode workflow and experience-ledger rules

- 2026-10-19 MCP toggle hardening (from live logs): disabling a server now cancels its connection-monitor task (the monitor used to report a false "Connection lost" one poll later and attempt a blocked reconnect) and tolerates a failing `close()` so the state still switches to disabled; toggle failures log the exception type instead of an empty message

- 2026-10-19 MCP toggle log classification: `run_coroutine_threadsafe` futures raise `concurrent.futures.CancelledError` (an `Exception`), not `asyncio.CancelledError` (a `BaseException`), so the callback now catches both and reports the actual server state instead of a misleading "toggle failed"

- 2026-10-19 MCP toggle state integrity: the SDK raises `asyncio.CancelledError` from `close()` when tearing down an anyio transport, which bypassed `except Exception` and left "config disabled but client still connected". The disable path now switches state and cancels the monitor in a `finally` block, and distinguishes an SDK-originated cancel from a caller cancel via `current_task.cancelling()`

- 2026-10-19 Built-in tool surface trimmed 53 -> 47: removed plan mode (`enter_plan_mode` / `exit_plan_mode` / `create_plan` / `get_plan`), tool discovery (`list_tools` / `tool_search`), and three registry-only entries (`execute_python` / `notebook_edit` / `search_codebase`). Deleted `tools/plan_mode_tool.py`, `tools/notebook_edit_tool.py`, `tools/tool_search_tool.py` plus the `ExecutePythonTool` / `SearchCodebaseTool` classes. `get_tools_definition()` and `TOOL_HANDLERS` now agree at 47; permission maps, concurrency categories, package exports, five test files and the prompt section "Plan-mode workflow" were updated to match. `tools/tool_registry.py` keeps its auto-registration path but no longer has a runtime consumer.

- 2026-10-19 Legacy `tools/` package removed. `TodoManager` and `QuestionManager` moved to `agent_core/state/` (`todo.py`, `question.py`) together with their dataclasses; the BaseTool-based tool classes and `ToolRegistry`, which had no runtime consumer, were deleted with the package. All seven call sites switched to the new module path (`agent_core.state.todo` / `agent_core.state.question`). Tests `test_grep_tool_async.py` and `test_tool_registry_autoregister.py` removed; the BaseTool section of `test_minor_hardening.py`, the registry branch of `test_code_agent_prompt_contract.py`, and the `QuestionManager` import in `test_clarify_dual.py` were updated. CI compileall/ruff no longer reference the `tools` directory.

- 2026-10-19 `browser_navigate` merged into `fetch_url`. Both were plain httpx GETs with the same User-Agent; `browser_navigate` never used a browser and its `extract_links` parameter was never implemented. `fetch_url` now takes `mode` (auto / text / raw): auto strips tags only when Content-Type is HTML, so JSON and plain text are no longer mangled by the tag-stripping regex. The RateLimiter (6 req/min per domain) and the PathDepthGuard pagination detection now cover `fetch_url`, and the per-turn cap was raised from 3 to 8 to suit lightweight fetches. `browser_navigate` removed from the schema, the dispatch table, the permission map, the chat_agent allowlist, the frontend status labels and two test assertions. Tool count 47 -> 46.

- 2026-10-19 Session auto-naming moved to the gateway. The old "first 50 chars of the first user message" naming is gone, so a session keeps the `"New conversation"` placeholder until the model names it. `gateway/session_title.py` produces the title: language follows the user message (CJK -> Chinese, otherwise English), 15 characters / 15 words, label + quote + trailing-punctuation cleanup, thinking disabled, two attempts with an 8s timeout each, and no billing entry (matching the previous title-gen behaviour). `_apply_generated_title` runs after `message.complete`, skips interrupted turns, returns early unless the title is still the placeholder (so later turns never spend a model call), writes with compare-and-set `update_title_if_current` (a rename made mid-turn wins), then pushes `session.updated`. Any failure leaves the placeholder and never affects the reply.

- 2026-10-19 Live generation rate in the status bar. `StreamConsumer` now keeps a cumulative output-token estimate (reasoning + content + tool-call arguments) and attaches it to every streamed event as `tokens`; the buffer is re-encoded with `estimate_tokens` only after 32 new characters, keeping encoding linear instead of quadratic. The gateway forwards the field on `message.delta` / `thinking.delta` / `tool.call_streaming`. The frontend derives tokens/s from those samples (2.5s sliding window, 500ms refresh, falls to 0 after 1.5s without deltas, reset when the counter restarts, stopped at turn end) and renders `N tok/s` at the head of the status bar's right cluster, left of the compress button — muted gold while generating, dimmed grey at `0 tok/s` when idle. Display-only: `usage`, `session_costs`, the context ring and the cost readout are untouched.

- 2026-10-19 Per-provider request shaping for OpenAI-compatible gateways (OpenCode Go). `agent_core/llm_client.py` adds `build_default_headers` (provider `extraHeaders` verbatim plus a `sessionHeader` whose value is the conversation uuid) and `apply_reasoning_mode` (drops the vendor `thinking` block when the provider sets `reasoningMode: "effort_only"`, keeping `reasoning_effort` — the shape the OpenCode CLI sends). All four client creation sites pass the headers: turn_executor (main stream), agent_chat_service_v2 title generation, gateway/session_title (new `session_id` argument), and gateway `_get_llm_client` (now takes a session id; `session.compress` forwards it). `gateway/config.py` returns `extra_headers` / `session_header` / `reasoning_mode` with empty defaults, so a provider that configures none gets `default_headers=None` and an untouched request body — the previous behaviour is unchanged. `generate_conversation_title` gained a trailing `conversation_uuid` parameter (it referenced the name without one, which would have raised at runtime). The settings-UI save path (`model.provider.save`) now carries `extraHeaders` / `sessionHeader` / `reasoningMode` over instead of rebuilding the provider without them.

- 2026-10-19 Request-shaping fields are editable in the model settings panel: `session hdr` (text), `reasoning mode` (`thinking` / `effort_only` pills) and `extra headers` (JSON textarea). The textarea is parsed on Save only, so a half-typed value never reaches the provider config; invalid JSON or non-string values show an inline error and block the save. `model.options` now returns `extraHeaders` / `sessionHeader` / `reasoningMode` so the panel can display stored values, and `model.provider.save` treats an empty value as a deletion.

- 2026-10-19 `maxEffort` provider option (`downgrade` default | `keep`). `build_thinking_params` gained a trailing `keep_max` argument, fed by `llm_client.keep_max_effort`, so a provider that accepts the top tier keeps `max` for non-DeepSeek models; the default still downgrades to `high` (MiMo and OpenAI reject `max` with a 400). Exposed in the settings panel as `max effort` pills and preserved by `model.provider.save`. OpenCode Go's GLM-5.3 declares `low/high/max`, so it needs `keep`; Kimi K3 declares only `max`.

- 2026-10-19 The four request-shaping fields in the model settings panel now sit below an `opencode-go` divider (rule with the label centered), so it is clear at a glance that they only apply to request-shaping gateways and are not required for the official provider APIs.

- 2026-10-19 Fixed: request-shaping fields never reached the main chat. `AgentChatServiceV2._get_llm_config` (the path `turn_executor` actually uses, which selects the provider that owns the requested model) returned only api_key/base_url/model/provider/temperature/top_p, so `build_default_headers` saw no `session_header` and sent no header — OpenCode Go answered 400 `MissingSessionID` even though the field was saved. The extraction now lives in `llm_client.shaping_from_provider` and both config paths (`gateway/config.py` by active index, `_get_llm_config` by model ownership) call it, so headers, `reasoningMode` and `maxEffort` travel with whichever provider is actually used.
- 2026-10-20 Unpriced-model usage: `_calculate_cost` returning None (model absent from model_pricing.json, e.g. OpenCode Go's deepseek-v4.1-flash) no longer suppresses the whole usage event — `_unpriced_cost_info` supplies real token counts with zero cost fields, so token totals, the context ring and call history work under subscription providers while the displayed cost stays 0.000. Cache-hit lookup also accepts the OpenAI-standard `prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens`. Request shape unchanged: the OpenCode Go gateway returns usage without `stream_options.include_usage` (verified against the live endpoint)
- 2026-10-20 Context-ring denominator: `MODEL_CONTEXT_LIMITS` gained a `minimax` entry at 1,000,000 so `minimax-m3.1-flash-free` (OpenCode Go) no longer falls back to the 128K default and matches `deepseek-v4.1-flash`. Vendor-prefix substring match, first hit wins, unmatched names still fall back to 128K. Display-only value: it sets the frontend ring denominator and does not gate compaction

- 2026-10-21 Tauri CSP hardening vs. the srcdoc preview. `tauri.conf.json` sets `dangerousDisableAssetCspModification` to `["script-src"]`. `dist/index.html` carries one build-injected inline script (the gateway token), so the default asset hardening adds a nonce-source to `script-src` and, per CSP3, any nonce/hash in a source list voids `'unsafe-inline'`. The srcdoc preview inherits the host policy but cannot carry a compile-time nonce, so in packaged builds every inline script the preview injects was rejected (html-to-image capture, preview bridge, feedback engine): the comment layer stayed inert and PNG export timed out after 15s with `failed`. Browser dev was unaffected because it runs without the Tauri policy. The `style-src` hardening is retained — `dist` has no inline style, so `'unsafe-inline'` there still applies and preview styling was never affected.
